            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>This Week's Observation: Budget Allocation is Polarizing</h2>
<p>This week, talking with several independent site teams revealed a clear trend: <strong>budget allocation is polarizing</strong>.</p>
<p>On one side, aggressive players continue to allocate budgets in ratios like 4:3:3 or 5:2.5:2.5 across Meta, Google, and TikTok. On the other side, conservative players directly concentrate 60%-70% of their budget on a single primary channel, leaving other channels with only basic exposure slots.</p>
<p>Both strategies make sense, but the question is: <strong>Do you know why you're allocating this way?</strong></p>
<p>Many teams make budget decisions by "shooting from the hip." When Facebook ad costs rose 15% last week, they hastily moved budget to Google; when a TikTok creative went viral, they rushed to increase investment—resulting in no stable models running across any channel.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Notable Changes This Week</h2>
<p><strong>1. Meta Ad Costs Continue to Fluctuate</strong></p>
<p>The aftermath of iOS 14+ persists. After Meta's attribution window adjustment, many sellers notice that conversion data "appears worse," but orders may not have actually decreased—it's just attribution delay or missing data. In this situation, rashly cutting Meta budget based solely on backend data might accidentally kill campaigns that are just starting to scale.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Prioritize cross-referencing data from third-party attribution tools (like AppsFlyer) rather than relying solely on Meta backend.</p>
<p><strong>2. Google Performance Max is Eating Into Independent Site Traffic</strong></p>
<p>Google recently intensified PMax spending in e-commerce categories, with automated bidding consuming more long-tail keyword traffic. If your independent site's SEO traffic isn't solid, you might notice a declining share of organic search orders, increasing paid traffic dependency.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Check your traffic structure. If paid traffic exceeds 70% of total, be cautious—this means your customer acquisition costs will become increasingly difficult to control.</p>
<p><strong>3. TikTok Creative Lifespan is Shortening</strong></p>
<p>Unlike Facebook, where campaigns stabilize after 2-3 weeks, TikTok viral creatives now have an average lifespan compressed to just 5-7 days. This means TikTok budget allocation logic is completely different: not "hold for the long term" but "fast in, fast out."</p>
<hr />
<h2>A Reference Framework for Independent Site Three-Channel Budget Allocation</h2>
<p>There's no one-size-fits-all formula, but you can self-audit based on these dimensions:</p>
<h3>Dimension 1: Your Product Decision Cycle</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>High decision-cycle products</strong> (furniture, 3C electronics): Users need 3-7 days to compare, suggest Google takes 40%+, as search intent is clear</li>
<li><strong>Low decision-cycle products</strong> (FMCG, fashion accessories): Facebook/TikTok takes 50%+, driven by creative-induced impulse purchases</li>
</ul>
<h3>Dimension 2: Your Creative Inventory</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Creative Count</th>
<th>Suggested Allocation Strategy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Less than 20</td>
<td>Concentrate on Meta testing; prove winners first</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>20-50</td>
<td>4:3:3 allocation, reserve 20% for testing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>50+</td>
<td>Consider 5:2.5:2.5, letting data models stabilize</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Dimension 3: Your Account Health</h3>
<p>If your ad accounts show these signals this week, budget allocation may need adjustment:</p>
<ul>
<li>Single account daily spend fluctuates over 30%</li>
<li>Multiple ad sets competing for the same audience, inflating CPM</li>
<li>Recharge frequency notably increases, but ROI doesn't improve proportionally</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>This Week's Action Items</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Complete this week's budget review by Wednesday</strong>: Don't just check ROI—verify spend stability and health of scaling ad sets</li>
<li><strong>Reserve 10%-15% flexible budget</strong>: Don't lock budgets completely; keep room to chase winners or emergency stops</li>
<li><strong>Monitor account balance alerts</strong>: Teams with chaotic budget allocation often have chaotic account balance management, leading to awkward "account runs out of money" situations</li>
<li><strong>Build cross-channel data comparison tables</strong>: Put Meta, Google, and TikTok CPA and ROAS on the same table for data-driven decisions</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Weekly Summary</h2>
<p>Budget allocation has no universal formula, but there's one core principle: <strong>let data speak, don't let anxiety drive decisions.</strong></p>
<p>Platform policies continue rapid adjustment this week. Rather than chasing hot topics and adjusting budgets, independent site sellers should first solidify their account infrastructure (attribution, creative library, account structure) before pursuing refined allocation.</p>
<p>Next week, we'll continue observing pre-Q4 peak season traffic changes. Stay tuned.</p>
